To answer your question, we need to look at the provided code snippet from `model_compression/model_compression.qmd`:

```tex
\begin{scope}[local bounding box=CIRCLE1,shift={($(CHANEL1)+(-5.6,0)$)}]
\foreach \i/\da in {1/,2/dashed,3/} {
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\y}{(2-\i)*1.5}
  \pic at (0,\y) {circles={channelcolor=OrangeLine,picname=1CL\i,\da}};
}
```

In this code snippet, the `\foreach` loop is used to iterate over the values of `\i` from 1 to 3. The `\da` variable is also specified for each iteration, and its value is set to `/` for `\i` = 1, `dashed` for `\i` = 2, and `/` for `\i` = 3.

Therefore, the 'dashed' option is specified for only one iteration, which is when `\i` = 2.

To compare the FLOPs count for the Attn × V operation to that for the QK operation, we refer to the provided textbook excerpt from `vol1/training/training.qmd`:

\begin{gather*}
\text{FLOPs}_{QK} = `{python} GPT2Compute.mac_factor_str` \times B \times N_{\text{heads}} \times S \times S \times d_{\text{head}}
= `{python} GPT2Compute.attn_qk_billion_str` \text{ billion FLOPs}
\\
\text{FLOPs}_{\text{Attn}\times V} = \text{FLOPs}_{QK};
\end{gather*}

As we can see, the FLOPs count for the Attn × V operation is equal to that for the QK operation, which is:

\begin{align*}
\text{FLOPs}_{\text{Attn}\times V} &= \text{FLOPs}_{QK} \\
&= `{python} GPT2Compute.attn_qk_billion_str` \text{ billion FLOPs}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the FLOPs count for the Attn × V operation is the same as that for the QK operation.
